The biggest problem
Your application mainly detects what the agent reports doing.

For example:

An agent sends:
{
  "agent_id": "payment-agent",
  "tool": "database",
  "operation": "read",
  "resource": "customer_records",
  "quantity": 40
}


Your detection engine can identify suspicious activity based on this event.

But consider this situation:
1. The agent executes a malicious shell command.
2. The command accesses a sensitive file.
3. The agent does not report this action through your gateway.
4. Your detection engine receives no corresponding event.
Your application may miss the attack completely.

This is the most important architectural weakness I would address.

You need independent security telemetry from the operating system, containers and network, not just telemetry generated by your agents.

. What I would build next to make this an advanced project
I would not replace SecRes. I would evolve it into a multi-layer security monitoring and incident response platform for AI agents.

The target architecture should combine five security layers.
Target architecture — SecRes V2
Layer 1 — AI Agent Security

LangGraph agents · Tool Gateway · Agent identity · OpenTelemetry instrumentation

Monitor tool calls, prompts, permissions, decisions, resource access and agent-to-agent communication.


Layer 2 — Host Security
Wazuh + Sysmon
Process creation, PowerShell, file changes, suspicious execution, system events.


Layer 3 — Network Security
Suricata
Connections, DNS, suspicious traffic, C2 indicators, data transfers.


Layer 4 — Container Security
Falco
Unexpected processes, shell execution, sensitive file access, container behavior.


Layer 5 — Application Security
Custom detection
Prompt injection, tool misuse, privilege escalation, agent behavior anomalies.




Layer 6 — Security Event Correlation

SecRes event normalizer + correlation engine + risk scoring + MITRE mapping

Combine independent security events into one incident.


Layer 7 — SOC / Incident Response
Wazuh
OpenSearch
Grafana
Incident API

Alerts · investigation · evidence · containment · incident reports


This is the architecture I recommend. However, do not install all these tools at once. Each one must contribute a different source of evidence.

